item.content:encoded:
    nullprogram.com/blog/2026/11/05/
  Buffered output typically comes in two flavors: fully buffered
and line buffered. If output goes to a terminal then the implementation
automatically selects line buffering, flushing its buffer when a line is
completed so that it reaches the user immediately. Otherwise it fully
buffers, holding output until the buffer is full or an explicit flush
event at some arbitrary point in the future. Line buffering is a common
bottleneck in non-interactive terminal applications such that it’s worth
redirecting output to a file or pipe in order to avoid it. It doesn’t
have to be this way. We can have our cake and eat it, too: promptly
delivering lines to a terminal at fully-buffered speeds.For example, Info-ZIP  prints file names as it extract them from an
archive. This printout dominates extraction time of archives with many
small files. Sometimes I run  without options, then my terminal
floods with file names and I know that my extraction is bottlenecked. So I
cancel and restart with  (quiet) to avoid the bottleneck, because it’s
faster to start over than wait. Conscious of this experience, I avoided
the problem in tugz without changing the user interface. You
get a listing but you don’t have to pay for it.$ unzip example.zip
Archive:  example.zip
  inflating: example.bmp
  inflating: example.svg
  inflating: example.txt
Consider this C program that prints three lines:()
{
    puts();
    puts();
}
If we examine the writes with, say, strace:$ strace -o log -e write ./a.out
a
b
c
We might see (here glibc):(, , )                      = (, , )                      = (, , )                      = Yes, glibc takes line buffering , even splitting writes
on a string. Some implementations are a little more sophisticated:(, , )                   = (, , )                      = CPython makes exactly those writes for this program: sys

sys.stdout.write()
sys.stdout.write()
Its rule is to flush if its buffer contains a newline after appending the
new output, with the consequence of possibly outputting partial lines.
Building on the previous example: {
    BUF_ERR  =  << ,
    BUF_LINE =  << ,
};

 {
      *buf;
     cap;
     len;
           fd;
           flags;
} ;

( fd,  *, );

( *b)
{
     (!(b->flags & BUF_ERR) && b->len) {
         (!os_write(b->fd, b->buf, b->len)) {
            b->flags |= BUF_ERR;
        }
        b->len = ;
    }
}
Such line buffering might be achieved with a test-then-flush:( *b)
{
     r = ;
     ( i = ; i < b->len; i++) {
        r |= b->buf[i] == ;
    }
     r;
}

( *b,  *src,  len)
{
     *end = src + len;

     (!(b->flags & BUF_ERR) && src<end) {
         left   = end - src;
         avail  = b->cap - b->len;
         amount = avail<left ? avail : left;

        memcpy(b->buf+b->len, src, amount);
        b->len += amount;
        src += amount;

         (amount < left) {
            flush(b);  
        }
    }

     ((b->flags & BUF_LINE) && has_newline(b)) {
        flush(b);
    }
}
If we’ve got a bunch of lines to print and append them one after another
into the buffer, that’s a lot of writes which could have been one.Let’s think about our goal: If the output is a terminal then we don’t want
to hold back lines indefinitely. That’s not the same as a write on every
newline. There’s already a solution for the problem where we’re in a hurry
to send out data, but the underlying,  caller makes lots
of small writes: Nagle’s algorithm.That is, flush the buffer if it’s been idle a short time. We don’t
actually care about newlines in particular. The catch is that it requires
tweaking the interface. Add a deadline to the buffer: {
       deadline;

} ;

();

( *b)
{
     ((b->flags & BUF_LINE) && b->len) {
         now = os_monotonic_clock();
         (b->deadline && now>b->deadline) {
            flush(b);
            b->deadline = ;
        }  {
            b->deadline = now + DEADLINE;
        }
    }
}
Where  is something appropriate for human perception, like 100ms
or so. We could poke this concurrently in a thread, awoken via futex when
data is written to the buffer. But better to poke it between short stints
of work, which is how tugz works. For example (using my strings): () {
         pre = S();
         suf = S();
        append(&stdout, pre.data, pre.len);
        append(&stdout, filename.data, filename.len);
        append(&stdout, suf.data, suf.len);

         () {

            deadline_flush(&stdout);
        }
    }
If it processes multiple files within the deadline, their names write out
together. No threads. The behavior difference compared to traditional
flush-on-newlines is imperceptible to a humans, aside from the fact their
program runs faster. One more reason not to bother with crummy C
stdio.

Invariants live in your code.Put preconditions, postconditions, and data invariants beside the code they describe. They become obligations the compiler checks.fn increment(n: usize) -> usize
    where n < usize::MAX
    ensures result > n
{
    n + 1
}A compiler that understands memory.Work with explicit layouts, ownership, and borrowed references. Evergreen tracks when a memory plane is live and which uses remain valid.let value: usize = 1;
let cell = &mut value;
*cell = 10;
fact same = value == 10;Powerful proofs that don't affect performance.Proofs, lemmas by induction, and ideal computations are erased after checking. Executable code keeps the work it needs to do.ideal let expected = integer::from(42);
let value: usize = 42;
fact matches =
    integer::from(value) == expected;
